  "response": "`crimes verdict` adds one thing to the picture, and it's a scoping fact rather than a ranking signal:\n\n```\n\"verdict\": \"unchanged\",  \"new\": 0,  \"fixed\": 0,  \"unchanged\": 42\n\"no new findings and no fixed findings\"\n```\n\nNothing here is a regression against `origin/main`. All 42 findings are standing debt, so \"fix now\" below means *next work session*, not *block the merge*. Nothing in this report justifies stopping a PR.\n\nTwo caveats on the input before the plan:\n\n- **The git-history category is empty, and not because the code is stable.** Every finding in the report carries `churn: 0` and `recency: 0`. The fixture has no usable history, so hotspot ranking contributed nothing to this ordering — the ranking is pure static signal. In a real repo, churn would likely reshuffle the middle of this list.\n- **Coverage warning:** `.crimes/suppressions.json` matched an exclude and was never read. Findings in it are absent, not zero.\n\n## Triage order (top 10 as ranked)\n\n| Order | Finding(s) | File | Category | Response |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `crime_00003` Missing Agent Context | repo-level | IA | **fix now** |\n| 2 | `crime_00001` + `crime_00009` (pull in 11, 17, 26, 27) | `src/date.ts` | correctness/temporal ¹ | **fix now** |\n| 3 | `crime_00004` String Sprinkles (`\"enterprise\"`) | 4 files, 4 areas | duplication | **fix now** |\n| 4 | `crime_00002` Return Shape Roulette | `src/petty.ts:21` | structural | **fix now** |\n| 5 | `crime_00005` + `crime_00006` (same destination) | nav + `billing.tsx` | IA / frontend | **defer** |\n| 6 | `crime_00007` Sync I/O in Hot Path | `src/api/files.ts:12` | correctness ¹ | **defer** (fold into ↓) |\n| 7 | `crime_00008` Option Bag Junk Drawer | `src/petty.ts:10` | structural | **ignore** |\n| 8 | `crime_00010` Negative Flag Maze | `src/petty.ts:35` | structural | **ignore** |\n\n¹ Four of the top 10 don't fit the five categories you named — they're temporal/runtime-correctness findings. I added a sixth bucket rather than forcing them into \"structural,\" which would have hidden what they are.\n\n**Why this order, and where it departs from rank:**\n\n1. **Agent context first** because it's a new file with zero blast radius, and it's the only fix that makes every *subsequent* fix safer — an agent editing `date.ts` or the nav registry without it has no way to know the conventions. It's also where the decisions the later items need (canonical time policy, canonical name for the billing destination) get written down. The finding is attributed to `src/api/files.ts`, but it's repo-scoped; that file attribution is an artifact, not a location.\n\n2. **`date.ts` as one job, not six.** The scan splits this file across six detectors (#1, #9, #11, #17, #26, #27), but reading the file, they're one defect: 52 lines with no time-domain policy at all. `dateLabel()` alone trips three of them — it mixes `getUTCFullYear()` with `getMonth()`/`getDate()`, and concatenates them with `+`, dropping both zero-padding and the zero-index correction on months. Fixing #1 (inject a clock) without #9 leaves the file still wrong; doing them together turns six findings into one commit. This is the single highest-yield item in the report.\n\n3. **`\"enterprise\"` before the nav work** because it spans `src/api`, `src/jobs`, `src/ui`, and `src/billing.ts` — four areas, four chances for the next agent adding a plan tier to update three of them. Cheap now, policy duplication later.\n\n4. **`resolvePaymentResult`** returns three shapes with 0% key overlap (`{ok, code, message}` / `{url, permanent}` / `{ok, value, cached}`) and no return type. Real, small, one discriminated union.\n\n**Deferred:**\n\n5. **`crime_00005` + `crime_00006` are the same defect** — `/settings/billing` is called \"Plans,\" \"Billing,\" \"Subscription,\" and `PricingPage` across five places. Mechanically trivial, but it's blocked on a product decision about which noun is canonical, and it's inert until someone edits one of the five. Do it as one change once the name is chosen — and note it's the shallow version of `crime_00018` (tenant/account/team/workspace across 5 files), which is the same disease at vocabulary scale.\n\n6. **Sync I/O** is real but shouldn't be its own commit — `loadPlanSchema` needs rewriting anyway for the override below. Fold it in.\n\n**Ignored:**\n\n7. **`crime_00008` is a false positive.** The finding says `describeCheckout` \"accepts a broad `options` object with an implicit shape.\" It doesn't — `src/petty.ts:1` declares `interface CheckoutOptions` with all six fields explicitly typed. The detector counted six property reads and inferred a junk drawer without checking whether the parameter was annotated. Ignore, and consider it a detector bug worth filing.\n\n8. **`crime_00010`** — `canRunBilling` is `if (!disableBilling && !skipRetry) return true; return false;`. Severity 0.25, the lowest in the top 10. It's readable as-is. Ignore unless someone adds a third condition.\n\n## Two rank overrides\n\nAggregate risk is a good prior, not a verdict. Two findings outside the top 10 belong above most of it:\n\n- **`crime_00016` Hardcoded Local Path (ranked 16) → promote to fix-now, position 1.** `SCHEMA_PATH = \"/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json\"`, and `loadPlanSchema` does `if (!existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)) return null`. On every machine except one, this function silently returns `null` instead of the plan schema — no error, no log. It ranks 16th because `agent_risk` is only 0.30, but it's the closest thing to an outright bug in the repo. Fix it together with `crime_00015` (`localhost:3000` in the same file, line 8) and `crime_00007` above — one file, one pass, three findings.\n\n- **`crime_00032` / `crime_00033` Contract Split-Brain (ranked 32/33) → fix-now tier 2.** These carry the highest severities in the entire report (0.90 and 0.96) and sink to the bottom purely on `agent_risk` 0.22. `Invoice` is declared in both `src/api/state.ts:9` and `src/billing.ts:19` and disagrees on **six** fields, including `subtotalCents`, `taxCents`, `discountCents`, `currency`, and `userId` — money and identifier fields, with nothing in the build checking the two against each other. If this repo were real, I'd rank this second overall, behind only `date.ts`.\n\nNet: five commits — `files.ts` portability, agent context, `date.ts` time domain, the `\"enterprise\"` constant, and the `Invoice`/`User` contract — retire 15 of the 42 findings, including every high-severity one.",
